<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hari Singh Nalwa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1791–1837</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hari-singh">Hari Singh</a> Nalwa was a commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Maharaja Ranjit Singh</a>. He took part in the conquests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kasur">Kasur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a>, and served as governor of Kashmir and later of the Peshawar frontier, where he built forts against Afghan attacks. He was mortally wounded at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-jamrud">Battle of Jamrud</a> in 1837 and is remembered as one of the greatest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> generals.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hari-singh-nalwa.jpg" alt="Hari Singh Nalwa" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hasan al-Din · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Painting_of_Hari_Singh_Nalwa,_by_Hasan_al-Din,_Lahore,_ca.1845-50.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
